Coefficient of linear expansion $\alpha$ is about one third of the coefficient of volume expansion:
$\begin{aligned}
& \therefore \frac{\Delta V}{V}=(3 \alpha) \Delta T \\
& \Rightarrow 0.003=3 \alpha\left(50^{{}^{\circ}} \mathrm{C}\right) \\
& \Rightarrow \alpha=2 \times 10^{-5} /{ }^{{}^{\circ}} \mathrm{C}
\end{aligned}$

A constant pressure air-thermometer is based on the law $\frac{V_1}{T_1}=\frac{V_2}{T_2} \Rightarrow \frac{T_2}{V_1}=\frac{V_2}{V_1}$ Here it is given that $V_1=47.5 \begin{aligned}$& V_2=67 \\
& T_1=0^{{}^{\circ}} $\mathrm{C}=273 \mathrm{ K}
\end{aligned} \begin{aligned}
\therefore \quad$ T_2=273 $\times \frac{67}{47.5}$& =385.07 $\mathrm{ K}$\\
& =112^{{}^{\circ}} $\mathrm{C}
\end{aligned}$

